## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to extend classic shortest-paths to dynamic updates with a concrete, real-world constraint (only decreases) and to analyze practical performance in terms of amortized work and memory.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic single-source shortest paths with only edge decreases
- Amortized analysis and potential-based arguments
- Priority-queue relaxation propagation
- Memory-time trade-offs and localized updates
- Practical optimizations: skip non-on-SPT edges, batch updates, integer weights with Dial's algorithm

## Code Example

```javascript
class DynamicSSSP {
  constructor(graph, source) {
    this.graph = graph; // adjacency: {u: [{v, w}, ...]}
    this.n = Object.keys(graph).length;
    this.dist = Array(this.n).fill(Infinity);
    this.dist[source] = 0;
    this.pq = new MinHeap(); // custom min-heap with push/pop
    this.pq.push([0, source]);
    while (!this.pq.isEmpty()) {
      const [d, u] = this.pq.pop();
      if (d !== this.dist[u]) continue;
      for (const {v, w} of this.graph[u]) {
        if (this.dist[v] > d + w) {
          this.dist[v] = d + w;
          this.pq.push([this.dist[v], v]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  decreaseEdge(u, v, newW) {
    // update edge weight in graph[u] item
    // assume newW <= oldW
    // attempt relaxation
    if (this.dist[u] + newW < this.dist[v]) {
      this.dist[v] = this.dist[u] + newW;
      this.pq.push([this.dist[v], v]);
      while (!this.pq.isEmpty()) {
        const [d, x] = this.pq.pop();
        if (d !== this.dist[x]) continue;
        for (const {to, w} of this.graph[x] || []) {
          if (this.dist[to] > d + w) {
            this.dist[to] = d + w;
            this.pq.push([this.dist[to], to]);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if many edges frequently decrease in a short window?
- How would you extend to multiple sources or all-pairs distances, and what would the complexity trade-offs look like?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design incremental geometry data structures, reason about dominance, and assess batch-update trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Pareto frontier maintenance
- Batch incremental updates
- Monotone frontier properties (x increasing, y decreasing)
- Trade-offs: update time vs rebuild cost

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch: merge batch into frontier
function insertBatch(frontier, batch){
  batch.sort((a,b)=> a.x-b.x);
  // prune intra-batch dominated
  let filtered=[]; let maxY=-Infinity;
  for(let p of batch){ if(p.y>maxY){ filtered.push(p); maxY=p.y; } }
  // merge with frontier (frontier: sorted by x, y strictly decreasing)
  let i=0,j=0; let newFrontier=[];
  while(i<frontier.length || j<filtered.length){
    let a = frontier[i], b = filtered[j];
    if(!a) { newFrontier.push(...filtered.slice(j)); break; }
    if(!b) { newFrontier.push(...frontier.slice(i)); break; }
    if(a.x < b.x){ newFrontier.push(a); i++; }
    else if(b.x < a.x){ newFrontier.push(b); j++; }
    else { // equal x, keep larger y
      if(a.y > b.y){ newFrontier.push(a); i++; j++; }
      else { newFrontier.push(b); i++; j++; }
    }
  }
  // optional prune to maintain y-decreasing property
  return pruneFrontier(newFrontier);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Analyzes streaming-frequency estimation with bounded memory and batch processing.

## Key Concepts
- Misra-Gries heavy hitters; counter trimming; candidate set.
- Trade-offs: accuracy vs memory; exact counting as baseline.
- Batch processing implications on update costs.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Misra-Gries skeleton
class MG {
  constructor(K){ this.K=K; this.map=new Map(); }
  add(x){ if(this.map.has(x)) this.map.set(x,this.map.get(x)+1); else if(this.map.size<this.K) this.map.set(x,1); else { for(let [k,c] of this.map){ this.map.set(k,c-1); } /* remove zeros */ for(let [k,c] of Array.from(this.map)) if(this.map.get(k)<=0) this.map.delete(k); } }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design incremental single-source shortest paths with batch updates and to reason about amortized work and practical data structures.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental SSSP with batch edge insertions
- Localized relaxation and pruning
- Priority queues and potential bucketed variants (Dial's)
- Amortized analysis and space efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
function updateBatch(batchEdges){
  for (const [u,v,w] of batchEdges){
    // ensure undirected view if needed
    adj[u].push([v,w]);
    adj[v].push([u,w]);
  }
  for (const [u,v,w] of batchEdges){
    if (dist[v] > dist[u] + w){
      dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
      pq.push([dist[v], v]);
    }
    if (dist[u] > dist[v] + w){
      dist[u] = dist[v] + w;
      pq.push([dist[u], u]);
    }
  }
  while (!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d, x] = pq.pop();
    if (d !== dist[x]) continue;
    for (const [y,w] of adj[x]){
      if (dist[y] > dist[x] + w){
        dist[y] = dist[x] + w;
        pq.push([dist[y], y]);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of streaming distinct-count with memory constraints and practical trade-offs between accuracy and memory.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog sketch
- register count m controls error
- per-element O(1) update, O(m) memory, mergeable sketches

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m = 16) {
    this.m = m; this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }
  hash(x) {
    let h = 2166136261;
    for (let i = 0; i < x.length; i++) h = (h ^ x.charCodeAt(i)) * 16777619 >>> 0;
    return h;
  }
  add(x) {
    const h = this.hash(x);
    const idx = h & (this.m - 1);
    const w = h >>> Math.log2(this.m);
    const leading = Math.clz32(w) + 1;
    this.registers[idx] = Math.max(this.registers[idx], leading);
  }
  estimate() {
    let sum = 0;
    for (const r of this.registers) sum += Math.pow(2, -r);
    const alpha = 0.7213 / (1 + 1.079 / this.m);
    return alpha * this.m * this.m / sum;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does choosing m affect relative error and memory? 
- How would you merge two sketches in a distributed setup? 
- What pitfalls exist with non-uniform ID distributions?

