## Why This Is Asked

This question blends sliding-window technique with binary search to analyze a threshold problem, testing practical algorithm design and reasoning about complexity.

## Key Concepts

- Sliding window for fixed-length subarray sums
- Binary search over the answer space
- Time/space trade-offs and edge-case handling
- Practical optimizations (early exit, optional prefix sums)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxLWithSumAtMost(A, T) {
  const n = A.length;
  const ok = (L) => {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < L; i++) sum += A[i];
    if (sum <= T) return true;
    for (let i = L; i < n; i++) {
      sum += A[i] - A[i - L];
      if (sum <= T) return true;
    }
    return false;
  };
  let lo = 1, hi = n, ans = 0;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
    if (ok(mid)) { ans = mid; lo = mid + 1; } else { hi = mid - 1; }
  }
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would the solution change if elements could be negative?
- What are the memory/speed trade-offs if you precompute prefix sums?
- How would you adapt for multiple thresholds T1..Tk in parallel?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming data structures, heap-based algorithms, and amortized complexity analysis in a real-time processing context.

## Key Concepts
- **Two-heap streaming median:** A max-heap stores the lower half; a min-heap stores the upper half.
- **Rebalancing invariant:** Heap sizes differ by at most one, ensuring the median is accessible in O(1).
- **Time complexity:** O(log n) per element for insertion plus O(log n) for rebalancing.
- **Space complexity:** O(n), proportional to total elements seen.

## Code Example
```javascript
insert(x) {
  if (L.isEmpty() || x <= L.max()) {
    L.insert(x);
  } else {
    R.insert(x);
  }
  rebalance();
}

rebalance() {
  if (L.size() > R.size() + 1) {
    R.insert(L.extractMax());
  } else if (R.size() > L.size() + 1) {
    L.insert(R.extractMin());
  }
}

getMedian() {
  if (L.size() > R.size()) return L.max();
  if (R.size() > L.size()) return R.min();
  return (L.max() + R.min()) / 2;
}
```
